  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What was the House of Wisdom?",
      "a": "A great library and centre of translation and learning in Baghdad, which grew under the Abbasid caliphs, especially al-Ma'mun in the early 800s. Historians still debate how big and organised it was.",
      "asks": [
        "What was Bayt al-Hikma?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why were books translated into Arabic?",
      "a": "Arabic was the shared language of a huge region from Spain to Central Asia. Putting Greek, Persian and Indian works into Arabic let scholars everywhere read, test and improve them.",
      "asks": [
        "What was the translation movement?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Who was al-Khwarizmi?",
      "a": "A Persian mathematician working in Baghdad around 820 CE. His book on solving equations gave us the word algebra, and the Latin form of his name gave us algorithm.",
      "asks": [
        "Where does the word algebra come from?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How did paper reach Baghdad?",
      "a": "Papermaking spread from China to Samarkand in Central Asia by the 750s, and Baghdad had a paper mill by about 795. Cheap paper made copying books much easier.",
      "asks": [
        "When did Baghdad get paper?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How did Islamic science reach Europe?",
      "a": "Through places where cultures met, like Toledo in Spain and Sicily. In the 1100s scholars translated Arabic books into Latin, and Europe learned algebra, Hindu-Arabic numerals and more.",
      "asks": [
        "How did Arabic books get to Europe?"
      ]
    }
  ],
